The equivalence derivations share an important boundary: the prelude executes before the width dispatch. A silent decline at that dispatch retains those prelude effects. `odata` starts at zero (D:212), and the common tail supplies the read result and returns success (D:411–422). Thus the zero-valued reads above do not acquire a distinguishing exception or return status.

For **#1/#3**, subsequent CRIME accesses overwrite REV before reading it. The interrupt and tick helpers do not inspect REV (D:98–189), so they cannot expose the intervening store. The ordinary-PC REV arm has no effect. For **#4**, that same inactive arm accompanies a zero high-word result.

For **#8**, CONTROL’s read arm is empty and its high word is zero. `acc ctl4r` and `p acc ctl4r` therefore remain nondiscriminating for this particular access. The nonzero `+0x0c` companion establishes a separate low-lane channel; it cannot distinguish a decline restricted to `+0x08`. This follows directly from D:289–295 and the exact-address switch at D:322.

For **#12/#13/#16/#17/#43/#44**, the written values contain no HARD_RESET bit. After DOG and ENDIANESS masking, residue is zero, so there is neither CPU stopping nor diagnostic output. Any register difference is erased by the next prelude; neither interrupt nor time processing reads CONTROL. This proves equivalence for both widths and both latch states. The `ctldog` and aggregate count rows can detect **incorrect masking**, but silently declining a write that correctly produces no text leaves their expected count unchanged.

For **#40**, the literal tuple write produces, when debugging output is enabled:

```text
[ crime: write to 0x4: 00 00 00 00 (len=4) ]
```

It changes REV’s low half only until the next prelude. Its address selects `default:`, so it has no REV redirect effect. `dflt ctl` counts **reads** at `+0x04` (P:681). The SET residual session writes a different value there (P:1713), but its rows at 1728–1738 never count that write diagnostic. Consequently #40 remains unrowed on its sole distinguishing channel.

For **#10/#11/#14/#15**, acceptance produces:

```text
[ CRIME_CONTROL: unimplemented control 0x0000000000000001 ]
```

The guest-state argument is the same transient-CONTROL argument, but these four accesses now have host-text oracles. `latch ctl msg` and `rearm ctl msg` each expect four occurrences; omission of one printing access removes one occurrence. They therefore belong in ROWED. These count assertions do not establish the diagnostic’s exact value or its free-running output channel; P:376–389 already distinguishes those limitations.

